From 0a99a865c6bbe0e0cd5d1b9f421189e796aef262 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Beri <jmberi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 10:16:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] drivers: display: esp32_dsi: wait for the GDMA bus clock
 before resetting

display_esp32_dsi_dma_setup() enabled the DW-GDMA bus clock and reset the
controller on the next line. Those are writes to two different peripherals,
and the reset faults with a CPU exception if it lands before the clock enable
has taken effect.

What makes this worth guarding rather than leaving to luck is that nothing in
the driver decides it. The same image faults or does not depending on how far
apart the two writes end up, so it moves with changes that have nothing to do
with DMA: samples/drivers/display runs, samples/modules/lvgl/demos faults at
the same instruction with a byte-identical DMA, display and PSRAM Kconfig set
and byte-identical generated code, and merely switching that build to
CONFIG_LOG_MODE_IMMEDIATE makes it run.

Read the clock back instead, using the predicate the HAL already provides.

Verified on a Waveshare ESP32-P4-Module-DEV-KIT (v1.3, 800x1280 JD9365):
the LVGL benchmark demo went from faulting every boot to running.

Signed-off-by: Jonathan Beri <jmberi@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/display/display_esp32_dsi.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/display/display_esp32_dsi.c b/drivers/display/display_esp32_dsi.c
index b256f0a47..ba6d9968b 100644
--- a/drivers/display/display_esp32_dsi.c
+++ b/drivers/display/display_esp32_dsi.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include <zephyr/drivers/display.h>
 #include <zephyr/drivers/mipi_dsi.h>
 #include <zephyr/kernel.h>
+#include <zephyr/sys/util.h>
 #include <zephyr/logging/log.h>
 #include <zephyr/multi_heap/shared_multi_heap.h>
 #include <zephyr/cache.h>
@@ -245,6 +246,20 @@ static int display_esp32_dsi_dma_setup(const struct device *dev)
 	uint8_t ch = data->dma_channel;
 
 	dw_gdma_ll_enable_bus_clock(0, true);
+
+	/* Enabling the bus clock and resetting the controller are two writes to
+	 * two different peripherals, and the second one faults if it lands
+	 * before the first has taken effect. Read the clock back rather than
+	 * assuming, using the predicate the HAL provides for it -- without this
+	 * the fault appears and disappears with unrelated changes to the image,
+	 * because what actually decides it is how long the two writes end up
+	 * apart.
+	 */
+	if (!WAIT_FOR(dw_gdma_ll_is_bus_clock_enabled(0), 1000, k_busy_wait(1))) {
+		LOG_ERR("GDMA bus clock did not come up");
+		return -ETIMEDOUT;
+	}
+
 	dw_gdma_ll_reset(dma);
 
 	dw_gdma_ll_enable_controller(dma, true);
-- 
